# Valgrind suppressions for the valgrind job in .github/workflows/native-checks.yaml.
#
# tools/ is in .Rbuildignore, so nothing here reaches the tarball or CRAN.
#
# One entry, and the leaked block is R's, not this package's. The mechanism is
# specific, and worth stating precisely because the obvious guess -- "R caches
# a buffer and never frees it" -- is wrong:
#
#   do_iconv() (src/main/sysutils.c) declares a local R_StringBuffer, fills it
#   via R_AllocStringBuffer(0, &cbuff), which takes the MAXELTSIZE default of
#   8192 bytes, and frees it with R_FreeStringBuffer(&cbuff) as its last act.
#   On the normal path nothing leaks. But when Riconv_open() cannot honour the
#   requested encoding, do_iconv() raises
#       error("unsupported conversion from '%s' to '%s'")
#   from inside its per-element loop. That free is a plain statement at the
#   bottom of the function, not an on-exit handler, so the longjmp skips it.
#
# So this is one erroring iconv() call, leaking exactly one 8,192-byte block --
# which is what valgrind reported, to the byte.
#
# Ours is the call that provokes it: xml_parse(..., encoding = "...") hands the
# name to iconv() at R/parse.R:48, and tests/testthat/test-encoding.R asserts
# that a bad name is a clean zuxml_encoding_error. That test is the trigger.
# Reaching R's leak by way of our own code is why this is suppressed rather
# than ignored: the block is unreachable from R's API and there is nothing the
# package can free.
#
# Two ways this stops being needed, if it ever becomes worth the trouble:
# validate the encoding name in R before calling iconv(), or wait for R to move
# that free into a cleanup handler.
#
# Written against the whole call path rather than fun:malloc alone, so it
# suppresses this one allocation site and stays silent if a real leak ever
# appears under a different caller.
{
   R-do_iconv-error-path-skips-R_FreeStringBuffer
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: definite
   fun:malloc
   fun:R_AllocStringBuffer
   fun:do_iconv
}
